      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "High-dose vitamin C can actually increase the risk of developing calcium oxalate kidney stones by breaking down into oxalate in the body. Taking lept 2,500mg to 3,000mg daily is generally considered unsafe and exceeds the tolerable upper intake level for most people, which can lead to serious side effects like kidney damage and nausea. Instead of high-dose supplements, it is much safer to focus on drinking plenty of water, limiting sodium intake, and maintaining a healthy calcium ratio in your diet. I strongly recommend consulting your doctor before starting any new vitamin regimen while you manage your kidney stones.",
